// Pirc Defence — the Watch course, one beat a move (WO-OPENINGS-REWRITE-01).
// Written from the fact sheet (`LESSON_FACTS=1 LESSON_IDS=pirc-defence`):
// masters' percentages, the settled engine verdict for each move, the board
// after it, and what the teaching computers found there. Standard:
// docs/OPENING-WATCH-BEATS.md, said in the think-out-loud register (§2a).
// The student plays Black. One story runs through every line: you let them
// build the center and then hit it from the side (…c5, …e5, …b5 — which one
// depends on their setup); the square e5 decides most of the fights (their
// pawn wants to push there, your pieces want to own it); and your king's
// address is a choice, never a habit (castle early against the Austrian,
// wait against the 150 Attack, and see what castling into the Byrne costs).
import type { WatchCourse } from './watchCourse';

const MAIN = ['e4', 'd6', 'd4', 'Nf6', 'Nc3', 'g6', 'f4', 'Bg7', 'Nf3', 'O-O', 'Bd3', 'Na6', 'O-O', 'c5', 'd5', 'Bg4', 'Bc4', 'Nc7', 'h3', 'Bxf3', 'Qxf3', 'a6', 'a4', 'b6', 'Qd3', 'Qc8', 'f5', 'Nd7', 'Qg3', 'Ne8', 'Qh4', 'Bf6', 'Qe1', 'Bd4+', 'Kh1', 'Nef6', 'Qh4', 'Qb7', 'Bh6', 'Be5', 'Rae1', 'Rfb8', 'fxg6', 'hxg6', 'Be2', 'b5', 'axb5', 'axb5', 'Rf3', 'c4'];

/** The main line's beats, in order — one per move of `MAIN`. */
const MAIN_BEATS: Array<[string, string]> = [
  ["They open with e4, the masters' most common first move, about 45 games in a hundred. In the Pirc you don't meet that pawn head-on. You let it build a center, and then you go after the center.", "e4 — the center you'll go after."],
  ["…d6 — the first move of the Pirc. Only about three master games in a hundred go this way; most play …c5, or put a pawn on e5 and fight for the center at once. You do something else. One small pawn move: it guards e5, so their e-pawn can't step forward for free, and it opens the diagonal for your bishop on c8. And then you let them have the center. Why? Because a big center is also a big target, and you'll hit it later from the side. The price is real: they get more space, and they are a little better.", '…d6 — let them build, then strike.'],
  ["d4 — of course. Nineteen masters in twenty take the second center pawn when it's offered. Two pawns side by side on d4 and e4: that's the center you invited. Now you need a first target, and one of those two pawns has no guard.", 'd4 — the center you invited.'],
  ["…Nf6 — and there's the target. The knight develops and attacks the pawn on e4, which nothing guards. More than eight masters in ten play it. That's how you develop in the Pirc: every piece comes out pointing at their center. So they have to spend a move guarding e4.", '…Nf6 — develops, hits e4.'],
  ["Nc3, about nine masters in ten: the knight guards e4 and develops. Why not f4 right away, the pawn they'd love to add to the center? Because e4 would still have no guard, and …Nxe4 just takes it. So the knight comes first. Remember f4, though.", 'Nc3 — guards e4 before f4.'],
  ["…g6 — and now it's really a Pirc. About half the masters play this; the Lion and the Czech are the other roads, and each has a tab of its own. What is the pawn for? It makes a home on g7 for your dark-squared bishop. From g7 the bishop will look down the long diagonal, straight through d4, at the heart of their center. That bishop is the piece this whole opening is built around. If the setup looks familiar, it should: it's a King's Indian, except their c-pawn is still at home on c2.", '…g6 — a home for the bishop.'],
  ["f4 — the Austrian Attack, the most ambitious thing they can do: three pawns side by side on d4, e4 and f4. It's the masters' most common choice here, about one game in four, and the engine's first choice. The idea is simple and a little scary: the e-pawn runs forward, kicks your knight off f6, and the whole center rolls over you. The quieter setups each have a tab of their own. Against this one, you have to be ready for that push from now on.", 'f4 — three pawns; e5 is the threat.'],
  ["…Bg7 — the bishop takes its diagonal; just about every master plays it. Right now your own knight on f6 stands in its way, but the day that knight moves, the bishop is looking straight at d4. And that's why you don't have to fear their push yet. If the pawn comes to e5, you answer …Nfd7, and three things are hitting it at once: the knight, your pawn on d6, and this bishop.", '…Bg7 — long diagonal, aimed at d4.'],
  ["Nf3, more than nine masters in ten. The knight comes out behind the pawns and adds its weight to e5, the square their whole setup is built to take. Why this square for the knight? On e2 it would shut in their bishop on f1.", 'Nf3 — develops, covers e5.'],
  ["You castle, as about seven masters in ten do; most of the rest strike with …c5 at once. Why castle first? Their center is about to open things up, and when it does, you want your king on g8 behind the bishop, and your rook on f8 already in the game.", '…O-O — king safe before the fight.'],
  ["Bd3, about two masters in three. Where does this bishop belong? On c4 it looks active, but your d-pawn steps up and hits it, and they lose time. On d3 it's safe, it gives e4 a second guard, and it clears the way to castle. Pushing on to e5 right now is the other try, about one game in eight; it has a tab of its own.", 'Bd3 — guards e4, ready to castle.'],
  ["…Na6 — a knight on the rim, on purpose. Masters split almost evenly between this and bringing the knight to c6. What is it doing out there? It's backing up …c5, the pawn break you're about to play: if they take on c5, this knight takes back. And why not the natural square, d7? There it would stand in front of your bishop on c8.", '…Na6 — backs up …c5.'],
  ["They castle too, nearly nine masters in ten, and the rook lands on f1, right behind the f-pawn. Both kings are safe, so now the real question of the opening gets asked: they hold the center, and every piece you have is pointed at it. Your move. Time to hit it.", 'O-O — king safe, rook behind f4.'],
  ["…c5 — the break you've been preparing, and practically every master plays it. The pawn hits d4 from the side. And here's what the knight on a6 was for: if they take on c5, it takes back, and it lands in the middle of the board. So they usually don't take. Watch what they do instead.", '…c5 — the break, hitting d4.'],
  ["d5 — they push past, as nearly every master does, and the center is closed. Look at the pawns now: yours on c5 and d6, theirs on d5 and e4. If you've seen a Benoni, this is the same picture, and the plan is the same too: you play on the queenside, where your pawns point, and your goal is to get …b5 in. Their play is in the center and on the kingside, where the e-pawn and the f-pawn want to advance.", 'd5 — closed center, a Benoni structure.'],
  ["…Bg4 — your last minor piece comes out, and it pins the knight on f3 to their queen. Why go after that knight? It's the piece standing behind their e-pawn's advance: trade it off, and the push to e5 loses a supporter. There's a sharper way to play here, a pawn sacrifice on c4, and it has a tab of its own.", '…Bg4 — pins the knight on f3.'],
  ["Bc4 — the bishop steps up to a square where your c-pawn can never hit it, because it's standing right in that pawn's way. From c4 it guards d5 and looks at your knight on a6. And the pin? They're not worried: if you take on f3, the queen takes back.", 'Bc4 — blocks …c4, guards d5.'],
  ["…Nc7 — the knight comes back from the rim. Why this square? On b4 a pawn just kicks it away. On c7 it does three jobs: it hits d5, it guards b5, and it clears a6 for your a-pawn. You can see the plan now: …a6, then …b5, and their bishop on c4 gets chased.", '…Nc7 — hits d5, clears a6.'],
  ["h3 — they put the question to your bishop on g4: take the knight, or go back? If the bishop retreats to d7, their e-pawn runs to e5 and your knight on f6 gets pushed around. So you already know the answer.", 'h3 — take the knight, or retreat?'],
  ["…Bxf3 — you take, and it's the engine's first choice. A bishop for a knight, but look which knight: the one that stood behind their push to e5. With it gone, that push has only the f-pawn behind it.", '…Bxf3 — removes the guard of e5.'],
  ["The queen takes back on f3, the engine's first choice; taking with the g-pawn would tear open their own king's cover. So they have the two bishops now, against your bishop and knight. With the center closed, that matters less than usual. But keep it in mind: if the position opens, the bishops grow.", 'Qxf3 — they get the bishop pair.'],
  ["…a6 — here's the pawn move the knight made room for. It prepares …b5, which would hit their bishop on c4 and start your queenside rolling. Can they stop the pawn coming?", '…a6 — prepares …b5.'],
  ["a4 — yes, they can. Count who covers b5 now: their a-pawn, their bishop and their knight, against your a-pawn and your knight. Three to two, so …b5 isn't playable yet. This is the fight of the whole line: you want that break, and they keep stopping it. So you bring more support.", 'a4 — stops …b5, three to two.'],
  ["…b6 — a small step with a real point. If their a-pawn reached a5 first, your b-pawn could never get past it: …b5 would simply be taken en passant. On b6 the pawn is safe from that. It guards c5, and it can still go to b5 later, with a rook or the queen behind it.", '…b6 — keeps the …b5 break alive.'],
  ["Qd3 — the queen lines up behind the bishop on c4, and the two of them point down the diagonal at your pawn on a6. That pawn has two guards, so nothing falls. But the queen has a second idea from d3: it can swing across to g3 and h4, toward your king.", 'Qd3 — lines up on a6.'],
  ["…Qc8 — one quiet queen move, three jobs. It gives a6 a third guard, against the battery. It looks down the light diagonal, through f5, all the way to their pawn on h3. And it's one step from b7, where it would stand behind the …b5 push. Do they let you get organized?", '…Qc8 — guards a6, eyes f5.'],
  ["No. f5 — they push anyway, right into the square your queen was watching. The pawn hits g6, and if you take it, the f-file opens for their rook, straight at your king's side. So you leave it alone. But this push has a price, and it's a big one: look at e5. Their d-pawn is on d5 and their f-pawn is on f5. No pawn of theirs can ever guard e5 again.", 'f5 — hits g6, but gives up e5.'],
  ["…Nd7 — and you take the square at once. The knight steps back, and now the e5 square belongs to you: knight, bishop and the pawn on d6 all cover it, and nothing of theirs does. Stepping back also opens the long diagonal, so your bishop on g7 finally sees all the way to their knight on c3. On h5 the knight would only be chased by a pawn.", '…Nd7 — takes over e5.'],
  ["Qg3 — here comes the queen, exactly as planned back on d3. From g3 it looks at two of your pawns, d6 and g6, and both are guarded. But g3 is only a stop on its way to h4.", 'Qg3 — looks at d6 and g6.'],
  ["…Ne8 — the knight leaves c7. On the queenside its work is on hold while their a-pawn stops …b5, and your king needs it more. From e8 it gives d6 a second guard, and it's one jump from f6, where it would stand in front of the king.", '…Ne8 — guards d6, heads for f6.'],
  ["Qh4 — and there it is. The queen hits two pawns again, e7 and h7, and this time one of them has no guard: e7. Your queen left d8 a few moves ago, and nothing else covers that pawn. How do you save it and get something for the move?", 'Qh4 — hits e7, which is loose.'],
  ["…Bf6 — the engine's first choice, and one move does both jobs: the bishop guards e7, and it attacks their queen on h4, which has no guard of its own. The check on d4 was tempting, but it would have left e7 hanging. And if the queen hops to h6, …Bg7 just hits it again.", '…Bf6 — guards e7, hits the queen.'],
  ["Qe1 — the queen backs all the way out. Three queen moves, from d3 to g3 to h4, and it's home on e1. From there it guards the knight on c3 and the pawn on e4. You've held the kingside, and it's your turn to do something.", 'Qe1 — retreats, guards c3 and e4.'],
  ["…Bd4+ — check, and the bishop lands in the middle of the board with tempo. From d4 it keeps watching the knight on c3. Can they block with Be3? Then …Ne5 jumps in and hits their other bishop on c4. So the king has to step aside.", '…Bd4+ — check from the center.'],
  ["Kh1 — the king steps into the corner, the engine's first choice. On h1 it's off your bishop's diagonal for good. Your bishop stays on d4, in the middle of their position, and now your knights can come back toward the center.", "Kh1 — off the bishop's diagonal."],
  ["…Nef6 — the knight comes back to f6. That was the square it was heading for when it went to e8. It hits both of their center pawns, e4 and d5, and it stands in front of your king again. Your pieces have found good squares: a bishop on d4, a knight on f6, a knight covering e5.", '…Nef6 — hits e4 and d5.'],
  ["Qh4 — the queen goes back to the kingside and looks at your knight on f6 and the pawn on h7. But look what it left behind. On e1 it was guarding the knight on c3; now only the b-pawn does. If you ever take on c3, their pawns get doubled on the c-file. Keep that in mind.", 'Qh4 — eyes f6, leaves c3 thin.'],
  ["…Qb7 — the queen arrives on the square it's been heading for since c8. Here it does two things: it adds an attacker to d5, down the long diagonal, and it stands behind the b-pawn. The break on b5 is getting closer.", '…Qb7 — hits d5, backs …b5.'],
  ["Bh6 — their last minor piece joins the attack, and it comes with a threat: the bishop hits your rook on f8. Notice where your dark-squared bishop is. It left g7 to go to d4, and h6 is a square it used to cover. So the rook should move. Does it?", 'Bh6 — hits the rook on f8.'],
  ["…Be5 — the bishop drops back to the outpost, the square no pawn of theirs can ever attack, and from e5 it still hits the knight on c3. But it's the wrong moment. The rook on f8 is still under attack, and the engine prefers …Rfb8 first: save the rook, and put it behind the b-pawn in the same move. After this they are clearly better.", '…Be5 — the outpost; …Rfb8 was better.'],
  ["Rae1 — they don't take the rook. Their last piece comes to e1 instead, behind the pawn on e4, and that leaves the a-file with no rook of theirs on it. Remember that. For now, your rook on f8 is still attacked, so you get a second chance to move it.", 'Rae1 — backs e4, leaves the a-file.'],
  ["…Rfb8 — now the rook goes where it belonged a move ago. It's out of the bishop's reach, and it stands on b8, right behind your queen on b7 and the b-pawn. Queen and rook are lined up behind that pawn, and the a-pawn stands next to it. The break on b5 is finally ready.", '…Rfb8 — saves the rook, backs …b5.'],
  ["They take on g6, and the pawn that has been sitting on f5 ever since they pushed it is finally traded. The f-file is open now, and their rook on f1 looks straight down it at your knight on f6. You have to take back. With which pawn?", 'fxg6 — opens the f-file.'],
  ["…hxg6 — with the h-pawn, the engine's first choice. Take toward the center: that's the rule, and here you can see why. The f-pawn stays on f7, where it guards the squares g6 and e6. Take with the f-pawn instead, and e6 becomes a hole next to your king, with the f-file wide open beside it.", '…hxg6 — toward the center; f7 stays.'],
  ["Be2 — the bishop gets off c4 before your b-pawn can hit it, so the break won't come with tempo. From e2 it still watches the same diagonal, through c4 and b5 to a6. They've seen what's coming. They just can't stop it anymore.", 'Be2 — steps away from …b5.'],
  ["…b5 — there it is, the break you've wanted ever since the a-pawn went to a6, and it's the engine's first choice. It took a knight retreat, a queen trip to b7 and a rook on b8 to make it work. The pawn hits a4, and whichever way they deal with it, lines open on the queenside, where your queen and both rooks are waiting.", '…b5 — the break, at last.'],
  ["They take on b5, and it's a mistake. The engine prefers pushing the pawn past, to a5, and keeping the a-file shut. Why does that matter? Remember where their queenside rook went: to e1. Once the pawns trade on b5, the a-file is open and only you have a rook on it. Just like that, you are a little better.", 'axb5 — opens the a-file for you.'],
  ["…axb5 — take back with the pawn. Not the queen: on b5 it would simply be lost to their knight. And now look at the a-file. It's open from a8 all the way down to a1, and your rook is the only one on it.", '…axb5 — and the a-file is yours.'],
  ["Rf3 — a rook lift with two jobs. Along the third rank it gives the knight on c3 a second guard, against your bishop on e5. And it clears f1, so the other rook can come over and double on the f-file against your knight on f6. They're still attacking. But the queenside is yours now.", 'Rf3 — guards c3, eyes f6.'],
  ["…c4 — the pawn steps forward and takes squares away from them. It shuts their bishop on e2 out of b5, and it freezes their b-pawn: b3 walks into a capture, and a two-square push can be taken en passant. You are a little better. They will double rooks against f6, and your knight on d7 and your pawn on e7 hold it. Your plan for the middlegame: push …b4 to drive the knight off c3, with your bishop on e5 already aimed at it, and bring your rooks down the open a-file.", '…c4 — cramps them; …b4 comes next.'],
];

const beats: Record<string, { say: string; sayShort: string }> = {};
MAIN_BEATS.forEach(([say, sayShort], k) => { beats[MAIN.slice(0, k + 1).join(' ')] = { say, sayShort }; });

/** A tab's beats from the first move no earlier line shares. */
type TabBeats = Array<[string, string]>;

/** Filed in this order: each tab's beats start where the lines before it end. */
